< A pretrained time-series foundation model can be added to a conventional forecaster at little architectural cost, but the resulting improvement must be separated from information-timing advantages and ordinary fitting variability. We study whether six statistics derived from a frozen \Chronos--T5-small model improve \XGB\ forecasts of the maximum 15-minute real-time hub-average price within the next ERCOT trading hour. Our design trains on 2019--2022, uses 2023 for validation, and evaluates on 8,767 hours in 2024. Both models use matched features, training rows, seeds, and hyperparameters; the augmented model alone receives \Chronos\ outputs generated from an explicitly delayed 512-hour price history. Under an assumed one-hour posting delay for archived real-time prices and realized load, the mean absolute error  is \$14.92/\MWh\ with \Chronos\ and \$15.07/\MWh\ without it. The paired improvement of \$0.15/\MWh\ is not statistically distinguishable from zero (95\% 24-hour moving-block bootstrap interval for augmented minus baseline MAE: [\$-0.32,\ \$0.06]/\MWh; bootstrap $p=0.131$; Diebold--Mariano $p=0.124$). This interpretation is unchanged by publication-delay, block-length, and target-completeness checks. A separate pre-2021-fit stress evaluation finds that both tree models severely underestimate prices during the February 2021 Texas electricity crisis, although they outperform latency-valid persistence outside that episode. Our results do not demonstrate a reliable incremental benefit from frozen \Chronos\ features for this target. They also identify two obstacles to stronger operational claims: archived settlement prices are not verified first-publication vintages, and the assumed availability of hourly realized load has not been established from historical posting records.
