< The threshold $y_t>\$200/\MWh\ identifies 114 of the 8,767 evaluation hours. The augmented forecast has spike precision 0.622, recall 0.202, and F1 0.305; the corresponding baseline scores are 0.559, 0.167, and 0.257 (\cref{tab:spikes}). While these point estimates favor \Chronos, both models miss most observed spikes. The difference is descriptive and is not a separately established statistically significant spike-detection improvement. The MAE on spike hours is \$562.28/\MWh\ with augmentation and \$565.56/\MWh\ without, compared with \$7.71/\MWh\ and \$7.82/\MWh\ outside the spike set. This contrast makes the loss distribution's tail sensitivity explicit.
